{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And when you bring, etc.</b> He said, 'Upon me is a meal offering baked in an oven'; and Scripture teaches that he brings either חַלּוֹת or רְקִיקִין — the חַלּוֹת mixed and the רְקִיקִין anointed. And our Sages disagreed about their anointing: some say he anoints and goes back and anoints until he uses up all the oil in the log — for all meal offerings require a log of oil — and some say he anoints in the form of a כִּי, and the rest of the oil is eaten on its own by the priests (Menachot 75a). Why does it say בַּשֶּׁמֶן בַּשֶּׁמֶן twice? To qualify second and third oil that comes from the olives, and first oil is required only for the Menorah, about which it says זָךְ (Exodus 27). And we learned in Menachot: all meal offerings are baked before their handful is taken and are taken by handful by means of breaking into pieces; all come as ten חַלּוֹת each, and the one about which רְקִיקִין is stated comes as ten רְקִיקִין (Menachot 76a)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "כִּי in the phrase מוֹשְׁחָן כְּמִין ״כִּי״ refers to the Greek letter chi (Χ), the shape of the cross-hatched anointing pattern described in m. Menachot 7:3."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:46.119441145Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}